string issueMaterials(string supervisorId, string issuesJson)
{
	// Extended for waste cut pieces, then for lots. An issue line is now:
	//
	//   { "materialId":"123", "qty":2.2,
	//     "cutWidth":55, "cutLength":55,
	//     "lots":[ {"lotId":"901","qty":3.0,"planItemId":"777"},
	//              {"lotId":"902","qty":2.0,"planItemId":"778"} ],
	//     "wastePicks":[ {"wasteId":"456","pieces":3,"planItemId":"777"} ] }
	//
	// cutWidth/cutLength/wastePicks/lots are absent on non-fabric lines and
	// everything behaves exactly as it did before.
	//
	// planItemId IS THE TONE PIN, and it is why the widget now decides the
	// mapping instead of this function inferring it. One sales order must be cut
	// from ONE lot or the finished garments visibly mismatch. Fanning a lot's
	// metres across whatever rows matched on material and cut size, in plan
	// order, let one order straddle two lots - the exact defect lots exist to
	// prevent. When a lot line names a Plan_Item, that lot's metres may only
	// close THAT item's requirement rows; when it does not, the old fan runs
	// unchanged, so an older widget keeps working.
	//
	// A lot may appear on SEVERAL lines with different items - two items of one
	// order sharing a lot is the normal case, not an edge one - so the pass key
	// below is the PAIR, while the stock check stays per lot.
	//
	// Rules worth stating up front:
	//  - qty may be 0 when wastePicks is non-empty. A requirement fully covered by
	//    waste needs no fresh fabric at all, and rejecting that was the old
	//    "quantity must be greater than zero" behaviour.
	//  - Pieces_From_Raw is recomputed here from the metres actually issued, not
	//    taken from the payload. The widget's split is advisory; this is the ledger.
	//  - For FABRIC, qty is IGNORED and the total comes from lots. The store
	//    person chooses which cloth leaves the shelf; a metres figure that did not
	//    say which lot it came from could not be received or disputed against one.
	//
	// ONE PASS PER LOT, and it is not merely tidiness. Cloth is usable only in
	// whole marker rows, so pieces have to be counted PER LOT and summed - never
	// derived from the combined metres. 500m at a 55cm cut is 909 rows in one
	// piece, but 300 + 200 is 545 + 363 = 908. Counting off the total credits
	// Pieces_From_Raw with a piece nobody cut, which is the same silent loss
	// CLAUDE.md already records four of. The per-lot pass makes it impossible
	// rather than merely guarded against.

	errJson = "";
	okJson = "";

	// DEBUG WRAPPER: Creator reports every runtime failure as a generic
	// "code 9430 - There is an error while executing Custom API." Catching it
	// here surfaces the real Deluge message in the widget console instead.
	try
	{
	supId = supervisorId.trim().toLong();
	issues = issuesJson.toJSONList();
	info "supId=" + supId + " issueCount=" + issues.size();

	// Oldest plan first, so material is consumed against the longest-waiting
	// order rather than whatever order the fetch happens to return.
	// Partially Received is still open - otherwise the materials for a plan's
	// unverified items could never be issued.
	plans = Production_Planning[Assigned_To == supId && (Order_Status == "Pending" || Order_Status == "Partially Received" || Order_Status == "In Progress")] sort by Priority_Key;
	info "plans=" + plans.count();

	// STEP 4 of the migration: one Material_Issue record per handover - per press
	// of Issue, however many materials it covered. The balances below say how much
	// is owed and how much has gone out; they cannot say that at 15:40 on Tuesday
	// this cloth crossed the counter. Lines are gathered here and written once at
	// the end, because a subform row needs its parent to exist first and the
	// parent cannot be created until we know there is anything to record.
	issueLines = List();
	primaryPlanId = 0;

	for each  issue in issues
	{
		// toJSONList() yields JSON objects, NOT Deluge Maps - .get() silently
		// returns null on those. .toMap() converts to a real Map first.
		im = issue.toMap();

		matId = im.get("materialId").toString();

		// NOTE: two more traps stacked here.
		//  1. ifnull() only guards null - a Creator field that was never
		//     written is EMPTY, not null, and toDecimal() throws on it.
		//  2. You cannot compare a number to "" - Deluge rejects mixed-type
		//     comparison. So normalise to string FIRST, test, then convert.
		qStr = ifnull(im.get("qty"),"0").toString().trim();
		if(qStr == "")
		{
			qStr = "0";
		}
		askQty = qStr.toDecimal();

		cwStr = ifnull(im.get("cutWidth"),"0").toString().trim();
		if(cwStr == "")
		{
			cwStr = "0";
		}
		clStr = ifnull(im.get("cutLength"),"0").toString().trim();
		if(clStr == "")
		{
			clStr = "0";
		}
		cutW = cwStr.toDecimal();
		cutL = clStr.toDecimal();

		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		// WHICH KIND OF REQUIREMENT THIS LINE IS FOR.
		//
		// The store screen shows the plan's own demand and reissues as SEPARATE
		// blocks, so the person ticking a reissue row expects that row to be the
		// one credited. Without this the fan below matched on material and cut
		// size only and filled whatever was outstanding in plan order - so
		// issuing 24 cones against the reissue block silently credited a
		// part-issued PLAN row instead, the reissue block still read as owing 24,
		// and the obvious next move was to issue 24 more.
		//
		// Fabric was not protected by the cut-size test either: a reissue copies
		// its cut size from the same BOM, so it matches exactly.
		//
		// Empty means "Plan" - a Creator field never written is EMPTY, not null,
		// and every row raised before Source existed came from the plan. A
		// payload with no source at all also lands here, so an older widget
		// keeps its previous behaviour against plan rows.
		//------------------------------------------------------------------
		srcWanted = ifnull(im.get("source"),"").toString().trim();
		if(srcWanted != "Reissue")
		{
			srcWanted = "Plan";
		}

		picks = ifnull(im.get("wastePicks"),"[]").toString().toJSONList();
		lotLines = ifnull(im.get("lots"),"[]").toString().toJSONList();

		matRec = Raw_Material[ID == matId.toLong()];

		if(matRec.count() == 0)
		{
			if(errJson != "")
			{
				errJson = errJson + ",";
			}
			errJson = errJson + "\"Material " + matId + " not found\"";
		}
		else
		{
			matName = ifnull(matRec.SKU,"").toString();

			// The human name, captured HERE - off a record already in hand, so
			// it costs nothing extra - and written onto the handover line below.
			//
			// It used to be left off, and Material_Name was filled with the SKU
			// instead. That made every handover line unreadable on its own, so
			// getStoreIssueHistory had to query Raw_Material again for each line
			// to recover the name. One query per issued line, every time anyone
			// opened History, to fetch something that was already sitting in this
			// variable at the moment the line was written.
			//
			// A handover is an audit record: it should say what crossed the
			// counter without depending on a second form to explain itself. It is
			// also the reason the name is stored rather than re-resolved - if a
			// material is later renamed, the handover should still read the way it
			// read on the day, and a live lookup would silently rewrite history.
			matDisp = ifnull(matRec.Material_Display_Name,"").toString().trim();
			if(matDisp == "")
			{
				matDisp = ifnull(matRec.Name,"").toString().trim();
			}
			if(matDisp == "")
			{
				matDisp = matName;
			}

			isFab = matRec.Is_Fabric;

			fabricWidthCm = 0.0;
			if(isFab == true)
			{
				// Stored as TEXT, in inches.
				fwVal = matRec.Fabric_Width_Inches;
				if(fwVal != null && fwVal.trim() != "")
				{
					fabricWidthCm = fwVal.trim().toDecimal() * 2.54;
				}
			}

			// Fabric is only ever issued as washed meters.
			if(isFab == true)
			{
				stkStr = ifnull(matRec.Wash_Quantity,"0").toString().trim();
			}
			else
			{
				stkStr = ifnull(matRec.Quantity,"0").toString().trim();
			}
			if(stkStr == "")
			{
				stkStr = "0";
			}
			available = stkStr.toDecimal();

			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			// Pass 1 - how much does this supervisor still actually need?
			//
			// Fabric rows are matched on cut size as well as SKU. Two plans
			// can want the same fabric at different cut sizes, and crediting
			// 55x55 pieces against a 45x90 row would silently close the wrong
			// requirement.
			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			outstanding = 0.0;
			outPieces = 0;
			for each  plan in plans
			{
				for each  row in Material_Requirement[Plan == plan.ID]
				{
					if(row.Material != null && row.Material.toString() == matId)
					{
						sizeOk = true;
						if(isFab == true)
						{
							if(ifnull(row.Cut_Size_Width,0) != cutW || ifnull(row.Cut_Size_Length,0) != cutL)
							{
								sizeOk = false;
							}
						}
						// Same rule as cut size: a row of the WRONG KIND is not this line's
						// row, however well the material matches. Empty reads as "Plan".
						rowSrc = ifnull(row.Source,"").toString().trim();
						if(rowSrc != "Reissue")
						{
							rowSrc = "Plan";
						}
						if(rowSrc != srcWanted)
						{
							sizeOk = false;
						}
						if(sizeOk == true)
						{
							reqStr = ifnull(row.Required_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
							if(reqStr == "")
							{
								reqStr = "0";
							}
							issStr = ifnull(row.Issued_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
							if(issStr == "")
							{
								issStr = "0";
							}
							rowRem = reqStr.toDecimal() - issStr.toDecimal();
							if(rowRem > 0)
							{
								outstanding = outstanding + rowRem;
							}
							if(isFab == true)
							{
								pRem = ifnull(row.Required_Pieces,0) - (ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Waste,0) + ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Raw,0));
								if(pRem > 0)
								{
									outPieces = outPieces + pRem;
								}
							}
						}
					}
				}
			}

			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			// FABRIC IS BUDGETED IN PIECES, NOT METRES.
			//
			// outstanding is the sum of Required_Qty - Issued_Qty, and
			// Required_Qty is written once at plan time and never topped up.
			// It is ceil(P/perRow) * cutL for the WHOLE order - ONE rounding
			// up. But cloth is only usable in whole marker rows, so every
			// separate handover rounds up again, and k handovers can genuinely
			// need up to (k-1) * cutL more cloth than the plan estimated.
			//
			// The old cap refused that difference, and the failure was silent
			// and permanent:
			//
			//   100 pieces, 3 per row, 55cm cut -> Required_Qty 18.70m
			//   issue 10.00m -> 18 whole rows, 54 pieces, 10cm thrown away
			//   46 pieces left need 16 rows = 8.80m; budget left is 8.70m
			//   -> 45 issued, ONE PIECE STRANDED FOR GOOD
			//
			// receiveMaterials gates fabric readiness on PIECES, so that item
			// never leaves Awaiting_Material and the plan never reaches
			// Material Ready. And nothing says so: the "nothing outstanding"
			// error needs outstanding <= 0 AND outPieces <= 0, so with a piece
			// still owed it never fires - Issue just does nothing.
			//
			// So for fabric the budget is RECOMPUTED from the pieces actually
			// outstanding. This is CLAUDE.md's rule applied to the metres:
			// Required_Qty is a pre-waste estimate, Required_Pieces is the
			// target. Issued_Qty may now legitimately exceed Required_Qty on a
			// fabric row - that is the model, not a defect.
			//
			// Only ever RAISES the budget. Whichever is larger wins, so a row
			// whose metres estimate was already generous is not cut back.
			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			if(isFab == true && outPieces > 0 && cutW > 0 && cutL > 0 && fabricWidthCm >= cutW)
			{
				perRowB = (fabricWidthCm / cutW).floor();
				if(perRowB > 0)
				{
					// * 1.0 first - an integer quotient truncates before
					// .ceil() sees it, which would budget one row too few.
					rowsB = ((outPieces * 1.0) / perRowB).ceil();
					pieceBudget = (rowsB * cutL) / 100;
					if(pieceBudget > outstanding)
					{
						outstanding = pieceBudget;
					}
				}
			}

			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			// Pass 2 - validate every waste pick BEFORE consuming any of them.
			// A half-applied issue would leave pieces missing from stock with
			// nothing booked against them.
			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			pickErr = "";
			// Two DIFFERENT counts, and conflating them is what stopped
			// Required_Pieces ever closing:
			//   physicalPcs      - waste pieces physically handed over
			//   piecesFromWaste  - CUT pieces those yield, which is the unit
			//                      Required_Pieces is measured in
			// One 300x400 remnant yields two 187x137 cuts: 1 vs 2.
			physicalPcs = 0;
			piecesFromWaste = 0;
			// The same yield again, split by WHOSE it is. A pick that names a
			// Plan_Item may only close that item's rows; one that names nothing
			// is spendable by any row the fan reaches, exactly as before. Two
			// buckets rather than one map with a "" key, because the free pool is
			// offered to every row and the pinned ones are not - putting them in
			// one map would need the "" entry read on every row anyway.
			wPinTot = Map();
			wFree = 0;
			// Dimensions and yield per pick, kept for the Issued movements
			// written further down. Keyed by waste record id - one pick per row,
			// so the key is unique.
			pkW = Map();
			pkL = Map();
			pkYield = Map();
			for each  pkRaw in picks
			{
				pk = pkRaw.toMap();
				wId = ifnull(pk.get("wasteId"),"").toString().trim();
				pcStr = ifnull(pk.get("pieces"),"0").toString().trim();
				if(pcStr == "")
				{
					pcStr = "0";
				}
				wantPcs = pcStr.toDecimal().toLong();

				wRecs = Waste_Master[ID == wId.toLong()];
				if(wId == "" || wRecs.count() == 0)
				{
					pickErr = matName + ": waste piece " + wId + " not found";
				}
				else
				{
					haveStr = ifnull(wRecs.Piece_Count,"0").toString().trim();
					if(haveStr == "")
					{
						haveStr = "0";
					}
					havePcs = haveStr.toDecimal().toLong();

					if(wRecs.Status != "Available")
					{
						pickErr = matName + ": waste piece " + wId + " is no longer available";
					}
					else if(ifnull(wRecs.SKU,"").toString() != matId)
					{
						pickErr = matName + ": waste piece " + wId + " belongs to a different material";
					}
					else if(wantPcs <= 0 || wantPcs > havePcs)
					{
						pickErr = matName + ": asked " + wantPcs + " waste pieces but only " + havePcs + " left";
					}
					else
					{
						physicalPcs = physicalPcs + wantPcs;

						wpwStr = ifnull(wRecs.Piece_Width,"0").toString().trim();
						if(wpwStr == "")
						{
							wpwStr = "0";
						}
						wplStr = ifnull(wRecs.Piece_Length,"0").toString().trim();
						if(wplStr == "")
						{
							wplStr = "0";
						}
						wpw = wpwStr.toDecimal();
						wpl = wplStr.toDecimal();

						// How many cut pieces do these actually yield? Same
						// grain-fixed maths as the allocation: across the piece
						// width, down its length, never rotated.
						//
						// A REMNANT TOO SMALL FOR THE CUT YIELDS ZERO, NOT ONE, and
						// the difference is a silent-loss bug of the exact family
						// CLAUDE.md already records four of.
						//
						// The fallback used to be a flat 1 for every case the guard
						// rejected, which lumped "this row is not counted in cut
						// pieces at all" together with "this offcut cannot take this
						// cut". The second credits Pieces_From_Waste with a piece
						// nobody can cut: the requirement closes, the item goes
						// Ready_For_Production, and the supervisor is holding a
						// remnant narrower than his marker. Fabric fulfilment is
						// counted in CUT PIECES - a row whose pieces read as issued
						// looks complete however little cloth it has.
						//
						// The widget never offers such a pick (remnantYield returns 0
						// and the scorer skips it), but this is a Custom API and the
						// payload is not evidence. The printed-piece block below
						// already gets this right - `if(pcAcross > 0 && pcAlong > 0)`
						// - and this is the same rule for offcuts.
						//
						// Zero rather than a refusal, deliberately. The remnant still
						// physically goes with him and its metres still leave the
						// rack; what must not happen is the requirement being told it
						// was covered. Under-crediting leaves the line open and the
						// store issues real cloth, which is the recoverable
						// direction - exactly what a too-small printed piece does.
						//
						// The flat 1 survives only where pieces are not the unit:
						// a non-fabric row, or a fabric row planned before
						// Required_Pieces existed and so carrying no cut size.
						yieldPer = 1;
						if(isFab == true && cutW > 0 && cutL > 0)
						{
							yieldPer = 0;
							if(wpw >= cutW && wpl >= cutL)
							{
								yieldPer = (wpw / cutW).floor() * (wpl / cutL).floor();
							}
							else
							{
								info "waste piece " + wId + " (" + wpw + "x" + wpl + ") cannot take a " + cutW + "x" + cutL + " cut - issued, but credited 0 pieces";
							}
						}
						piecesFromWaste = piecesFromWaste + (wantPcs * yieldPer);

						pkPin = ifnull(pk.get("planItemId"),"").toString().trim();
						if(pkPin == "")
						{
							wFree = wFree + (wantPcs * yieldPer);
						}
						else
						{
							wpPrev = ifnull(wPinTot.get(pkPin),"0").toString().trim();
							if(wpPrev == "")
							{
								wpPrev = "0";
							}
							wPinTot.put(pkPin,wpPrev.toLong() + (wantPcs * yieldPer));
						}

						pkW.put(wId,wpw);
						pkL.put(wId,wpl);
						pkYield.put(wId,yieldPer);
					}
				}
			}

			// A remnant can yield more cuts than are still needed. The surplus
			// is not booked against the requirement - it becomes waste again
			// once the supervisor has cut what he needs.
			//
			// wFree/wPinTot are deliberately NOT trimmed to match. They say what
			// each row is ALLOWED to draw on, not how much exists; the running
			// total below is the one that says that, and every give is capped
			// against it. Trimming a total across two buckets would need a rule
			// for which bucket loses, and there isn't an honest one.
			if(piecesFromWaste > outPieces)
			{
				piecesFromWaste = outPieces;
			}

			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			// Pass 2b - VALIDATE EVERY LOT BEFORE CONSUMING ANY.
			//
			// Same rule as the waste picks above: a half-applied issue takes
			// cloth off a lot with nothing booked against it, and there is no
			// transaction in Deluge to roll that back with.
			//
			// A lot line is checked for: existing, belonging to THIS material,
			// not Blocked, and holding enough WASHED cloth. Fabric only ever
			// leaves as washed metres, so Wash_Quantity is the figure - a lot
			// full of unwashed cloth is not issuable however much it holds.
			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			//
			// TWO KEYS, and they are not the same key.
			//   lotOrder / lotSum / lotHave / lotName - per LOT, because stock is
			//     held by the lot and a lot named on three lines can only give
			//     what it has once.
			//   passOrder / lotWant / lotOf / pinOf   - per LOT x PLAN ITEM,
			//     because that pair is what a pass may fan into. Two items of one
			//     order sharing a lot is the ordinary case, and summing them onto
			//     the lot would put the pair back into one pass with no way to
			//     say which item each metre was for.
			// A line with no planItemId keys on the lot alone (pin ""), so an
			// older widget produces exactly one pass per lot, as it always did.
			//
			lotOrder = List();
			passOrder = List();
			lotSum = Map();
			lotWant = Map();
			lotOf = Map();
			pinOf = Map();
			noteOf = Map();
			fromOf = Map();
			lotHave = Map();
			lotName = Map();
			// Pass key -> "pieceId:count,pieceId:count". Empty for every roll
			// lot, so the whole piece path below is unreachable on the flow that
			// existed before printing.
			passPieces = Map();
			// ONE ORDER, ONE LOT - checked here rather than trusted.
			//
			// The widget allocates per order and cannot produce a straddling
			// order, but a Custom API is callable from anywhere and the guarantee
			// is worth exactly as much as its weakest caller. Two shades in one
			// order is the defect lots exist to prevent, and unlike a stock
			// shortfall it cannot be undone once the cloth is cut.
			//
			// planId -> the lot that order is taking from. Empty on an older
			// widget, which then behaves exactly as it did before.
			orderLot = Map();
			lotErr = "";
			lotTotal = 0.0;

			for each  laRaw in lotLines
			{
				la = laRaw.toMap();
				laId = ifnull(la.get("lotId"),"").toString().trim();
				laPin = ifnull(la.get("planItemId"),"").toString().trim();
				laOrd = ifnull(la.get("planId"),"").toString().trim();

				laqS = ifnull(la.get("qty"),"0").toString().trim();
				if(laqS == "")
				{
					laqS = "0";
				}
				laQ = laqS.toDecimal();

				// A zero line is not an error - the widget sends a row per lot
				// on screen and he only fills the ones he is taking from.
				if(laQ > 0)
				{
					if(laId == "")
					{
						lotErr = matName + ": a lot line arrived with no lot";
					}
					else
					{
						// The SAME lot twice in one payload is summed, not
						// treated as two lines. Two rows against one lot would
						// each be checked against the full stock separately and
						// could together take more than it holds. This is the
						// LOT total and stays the lot total even when the lines
						// are for different items - the shelf does not care who
						// the cloth is for.
						prevWant = ifnull(lotSum.get(laId),"0").toString().trim();
						if(prevWant == "")
						{
							prevWant = "0";
						}

						if(prevWant.toDecimal() == 0)
						{
							lotRecs2 = Raw_Material_Lot[ID == laId.toLong()];
							if(lotRecs2.count() == 0)
							{
								lotErr = matName + ": lot " + laId + " not found";
							}
							else
							{
								for each  lrec in lotRecs2
								{
									if(ifnull(lrec.Material,"").toString().trim() != matId)
									{
										lotErr = matName + ": lot " + laId + " belongs to a different material";
									}
									else if(ifnull(lrec.Status,"Active").toString().trim() == "Blocked")
									{
										lotErr = matName + ": lot " + ifnull(lrec.Lot_Number,laId).toString() + " is blocked";
									}
									else
									{
										lhS = ifnull(lrec.Wash_Quantity,"0").toString().trim();
										if(lhS == "")
										{
											lhS = "0";
										}
										lotHave.put(laId,lhS.toDecimal());
										lotName.put(laId,ifnull(lrec.Lot_Number,laId).toString());
										lotOrder.add(laId);
									}
								}
							}
						}

						lotSum.put(laId,prevWant.toDecimal() + laQ);

						// THE ORDER MAY NAME ONLY ONE LOT. Two lines of the same
						// order against two lots is rejected outright - the whole
						// material line is refused rather than one of the two
						// silently winning, because there is no way to tell from
						// here which shade the finished pieces should be.
						if(laOrd != "")
						{
							prevOrdLot = ifnull(orderLot.get(laOrd),"").toString().trim();
							if(prevOrdLot == "")
							{
								orderLot.put(laOrd,laId);
							}
							else if(prevOrdLot != laId)
							{
								lotErr = matName + ": one order cannot be cut from two lots";
							}
						}

						// The pass this line belongs to. Registered once, in
						// payload order, so a lot split across two items still
						// gets one pass each and the metres are never merged.
						passKey = laId + "|" + laPin;
						prevPass = ifnull(lotWant.get(passKey),"0").toString().trim();
						if(prevPass == "")
						{
							prevPass = "0";
						}
						if(prevPass.toDecimal() == 0)
						{
							passOrder.add(passKey);
							lotOf.put(passKey,laId);
							pinOf.put(passKey,laPin);
							// A DELIBERATE TONE OVERRIDE, and the only record of it.
							//
							// The order was cut from one lot, that lot ran out, and
							// the store person accepted a visible mismatch to finish
							// it. Issued_Lot deliberately keeps the ORIGINAL, so
							// without this the handover shows a different lot and
							// nothing anywhere says a person chose that rather than a
							// rule slipping. Empty on every ordinary issue.
							noteOf.put(passKey,ifnull(la.get("note"),"").toString().trim().replaceAll("\"","'"));
							fromOf.put(passKey,ifnull(la.get("overrideFrom"),"").toString().trim());
						}
						lotWant.put(passKey,prevPass.toDecimal() + laQ);

						//--------------------------------------------------
						// THE PHYSICAL PIECES, on a lot held as pieces.
						//
						// COLLECTED, NEVER DERIVED. Three 3.00 m pieces are
						// 9.00 m, and 9.00 m against a 55 cm cut divides to
						// floor(900/55) = 16 marker rows - but each piece is
						// cut on its own and yields floor(300/55) = 5, so the
						// truth is 15. Crediting the 16th is a piece nobody can
						// cut: the requirement closes early, the item never
						// leaves Awaiting_Material, and pressing Issue again
						// does nothing because the "nothing outstanding" test
						// needs both metres and pieces spent. That is the same
						// silent-loss shape CLAUDE.md already records four of.
						//
						// Held as two parallel lists per pass, flattened into
						// "id:count" strings - Deluge has no nested collection
						// that survives a Map value cleanly, and this is read
						// back exactly once, in the pass loop.
						pcRaw = ifnull(la.get("pieces"),"[]").toString().toJSONList();

						if(pcRaw.size() > 0)
						{
							prevPc = ifnull(passPieces.get(passKey),"").toString();

							for each  pcOne in pcRaw
							{
								pcM = pcOne.toMap();
								pcIdTxt = ifnull(pcM.get("pieceId"),"").toString().trim();
								pcCntS = ifnull(pcM.get("count"),"0").toString().trim();
								if(pcCntS == "")
								{
									pcCntS = "0";
								}

								if(pcIdTxt != "" && pcCntS.toDecimal() > 0)
								{
									if(prevPc != "")
									{
										prevPc = prevPc + ",";
									}
									prevPc = prevPc + pcIdTxt + ":" + pcCntS.toDecimal();
								}
							}

							passPieces.put(passKey,prevPc);
						}

						lotTotal = lotTotal + laQ;
					}
				}
			}

			// Stock check AFTER summing, so a lot named twice is measured once
			// against what it actually holds. Against lotSum, NOT lotWant - the
			// per-pass figures are shares of this and each one is under the
			// lot's stock on its own even when together they are not.
			for each  lchkId in lotOrder
			{
				wantHere = ifnull(lotSum.get(lchkId),"0").toString().trim();
				if(wantHere == "")
				{
					wantHere = "0";
				}
				haveHere = ifnull(lotHave.get(lchkId),"0").toString().trim();
				if(haveHere == "")
				{
					haveHere = "0";
				}
				if(wantHere.toDecimal() > haveHere.toDecimal())
				{
					lotErr = matName + ": lot " + ifnull(lotName.get(lchkId),lchkId).toString() + " has " + haveHere.toDecimal() + " washed, asked " + wantHere.toDecimal();
				}
			}

			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			// FOR FABRIC THE LOTS ARE THE ASK. The payload's qty is advisory
			// and is overridden, for the same reason Pieces_From_Raw is
			// recomputed rather than trusted: this function is the ledger.
			//
			// Fabric with no lot at all is REFUSED rather than issued off the
			// parent total. Letting it through would move Raw_Material without
			// moving any lot, so the parent and the sum of its lots would
			// silently disagree - and nothing downstream could say which cloth
			// the supervisor is holding, which is what receipt and disputes
			// both need.
			//--------------------------------------------------------------
			if(isFab == true)
			{
				askQty = lotTotal;
				if(lotErr == "" && lotTotal <= 0 && picks.size() == 0)
				{
					lotErr = matName + ": choose which lot the cloth comes from";
				}
			}

			if(askQty < 0)
			{
				if(errJson != "")
				{
					errJson = errJson + ",";
				}
				errJson = errJson + "\"" + matName + ": quantity cannot be negative\"";
			}
			else if(askQty == 0 && picks.size() == 0)
			{
				if(errJson != "")
				{
					errJson = errJson + ",";
				}
				errJson = errJson + "\"" + matName + ": nothing to issue\"";
			}
			else if(pickErr != "")
			{
				if(errJson != "")
				{
					errJson = errJson + ",";
				}
				errJson = errJson + "\"" + pickErr.replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"";
			}
			else if(lotErr != "")
			{
				if(errJson != "")
				{
					errJson = errJson + ",";
				}
				errJson = errJson + "\"" + lotErr.replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"";
			}
			else if(outstanding <= 0 && outPieces <= 0)
			{
				if(errJson != "")
				{
					errJson = errJson + ",";
				}
				errJson = errJson + "\"" + matName + ": nothing outstanding for this supervisor\"";
			}
			else if(askQty > available)
			{
				if(errJson != "")
				{
					errJson = errJson + ",";
				}
				errJson = errJson + "\"" + matName + ": asked " + askQty + " but only " + available + " in stock\"";
			}
			else
			{
				// Never issue more than the supervisor still needs - the extra
				// would decrement real stock with nowhere to book it.
				issueQty = askQty;
				if(issueQty > outstanding)
				{
					issueQty = outstanding;
				}

				//----------------------------------------------------------
				// Consume the waste pieces. Validation already passed, so
				// this loop cannot fail partway.
				//----------------------------------------------------------
				for each  pkRaw2 in picks
				{
					pk2 = pkRaw2.toMap();
					wId2 = ifnull(pk2.get("wasteId"),"").toString().trim();
					pcStr2 = ifnull(pk2.get("pieces"),"0").toString().trim();
					if(pcStr2 == "")
					{
						pcStr2 = "0";
					}
					takePcs = pcStr2.toDecimal().toLong();

					for each  wRec in Waste_Master[ID == wId2.toLong()]
					{
						haveStr2 = ifnull(wRec.Piece_Count,"0").toString().trim();
						if(haveStr2 == "")
						{
							haveStr2 = "0";
						}
						transStr2 = ifnull(wRec.In_Transit_Count,"0").toString().trim();
						if(transStr2 == "")
						{
							transStr2 = "0";
						}
						leftPcs = haveStr2.toDecimal().toLong() - takePcs;

						// Handed over, NOT yet consumed. The pieces leave the
						// rack so nobody can be offered them twice, but they do
						// not leave the company until the supervisor confirms
						// he is holding them. Between those two moments they
						// are in transit and belong to neither side.
						wRec.Piece_Count = leftPcs;
						wRec.In_Transit_Count = transStr2.toDecimal().toLong() + takePcs;

						// A piece is always consumed whole, so an emptied row
						// is off the rack - but "Issued" not "Consumed", which
						// now means confirmed received.
						if(leftPcs <= 0)
						{
							wRec.Status = "Issued";
						}
					}
				}

				//----------------------------------------------------------
				// THE PASSES - one per lot the cloth comes off, and per item
				// when the widget named one.
				//
				// Non-fabric gets a single pass with an empty key, so it walks
				// exactly the same code and behaves exactly as before.
				//----------------------------------------------------------
				passIds = List();
				if(isFab == true)
				{
					for each  lpId in passOrder
					{
						passIds.add(lpId);
					}

					// A FABRIC LINE COVERED ENTIRELY BY OFFCUTS HAS NO LOT LINE,
					// so it had no pass at all - and the fan that credits
					// Pieces_From_Waste lives inside the pass loop.
					//
					// The remnants were taken off the rack and moved to
					// In_Transit_Count above regardless, so they left the store
					// with NOTHING booking them: the requirement stayed open at
					// its full piece count and the pieces were gone. Silent, and
					// the okJson line even reported piecesFromWaste as though it
					// had been credited.
					//
					// One pass carrying no lot moves no cloth - thisQty comes out
					// 0 and the stock block below is skipped - and lets the waste
					// credit land where it belongs.
					if(passIds.size() == 0 && picks.size() > 0)
					{
						passIds.add("");
					}
				}
				else
				{
					passIds.add("");
				}

				// Waste credit belongs to the ISSUE, not to any one lot, so it
				// is carried across the passes and spent by the first rows that
				// need it. The second pass simply finds it already spent.
				fanWaste = piecesFromWaste;
				// A physical remnant cannot be split across two plans, so the
				// Issued movements are attached to the FIRST plan that takes
				// waste credit for this material and cut size, and carry that
				// row's Plan_Item. If an issue happens to span two plans the
				// handover is recorded against the older one. Set OUTSIDE the
				// pass loop so a two-lot issue still logs them exactly once.
				wasteLogged = false;
				piecesFromRawTotal = 0;
				issuedTotal = 0.0;
				// Declared out here as well as inside the guard below: a pinned
				// pass whose item turns out to need nothing is skipped entirely,
				// and with every pass skipped the okJson line at the end would
				// otherwise read a variable that was never assigned.
				piecesFromRaw = 0;

				perRowP = 0;
				canPiece = false;
				if(isFab == true && cutW > 0 && cutL > 0 && fabricWidthCm >= cutW)
				{
					perRowP = (fabricWidthCm / cutW).floor();
					if(perRowP > 0)
					{
						canPiece = true;
					}
				}

				for each  passId in passIds
				{
					//------------------------------------------------------
					// WHICH LOT, AND FOR WHOM.
					//
					// passId is the pass key, not the lot - the lot has to come
					// back out of it for the stock movement and the handover
					// line, both of which are about cloth and not about items.
					// Non-fabric has neither, and both read back as "".
					//------------------------------------------------------
					passLot = ifnull(lotOf.get(passId),"").toString().trim();
					passPin = ifnull(pinOf.get(passId),"").toString().trim();

					//------------------------------------------------------
					// A PINNED PASS IS BUDGETED AGAINST ITS OWN ITEM, LIVE.
					//
					// The ceilings further down - how many metres this pass may
					// take, how many pieces its cloth may be credited with - are
					// computed from what is still owed. Unpinned that is the
					// whole line's outstanding, held in outPieces/outstanding
					// from pass 1. Pinned it must be THIS ITEM's, and using the
					// line's total instead is not a cosmetic overstatement:
					//
					//   A and B each owe 10 pieces at 2 per row. The widget sends
					//   5.50m for A and 5.50m for B. A's pass sees 20 pieces
					//   outstanding, so nothing caps it, counts its 5.50m as 20
					//   pieces of credit, and books 10 of them - A's rows will
					//   take no more. B's pass then reads 0 pieces still owed,
					//   caps its metres at 0 and moves nothing. B is left owing
					//   everything, with no error, and the 2.75m of A's cloth
					//   that no row absorbed has left the lot unbooked.
					//
					// Read off the RECORDS rather than kept in a running map,
					// because earlier passes have already written Issued_Qty,
					// Pieces_From_Raw and Pieces_From_Waste onto them - the rows
					// are the tally, and a second tally beside them would be one
					// more thing to keep in step. Costs one scan per pinned pass,
					// the same scan the fan below already does.
					//------------------------------------------------------
					pinPcsLeft = 0;
					pinQtyLeft = 0.0;
					if(passPin != "")
					{
						for each  plan in plans
						{
							for each  row in Material_Requirement[Plan == plan.ID]
							{
								if(row.Material != null && row.Material.toString() == matId)
								{
									pinOk = true;
									if(isFab == true)
									{
										if(ifnull(row.Cut_Size_Width,0) != cutW || ifnull(row.Cut_Size_Length,0) != cutL)
										{
											pinOk = false;
										}
									}
									rowSrcP = ifnull(row.Source,"").toString().trim();
									if(rowSrcP != "Reissue")
									{
										rowSrcP = "Plan";
									}
									if(rowSrcP != srcWanted)
									{
										pinOk = false;
									}
									// Lookup fields hold record ids, so this is an
									// id against the id the payload carried.
									if(ifnull(row.Plan_Item,"").toString().trim() != passPin)
									{
										pinOk = false;
									}
									if(pinOk == true)
									{
										reqStrP = ifnull(row.Required_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
										if(reqStrP == "")
										{
											reqStrP = "0";
										}
										issStrP = ifnull(row.Issued_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
										if(issStrP == "")
										{
											issStrP = "0";
										}
										remP = reqStrP.toDecimal() - issStrP.toDecimal();
										if(remP > 0)
										{
											pinQtyLeft = pinQtyLeft + remP;
										}
										if(isFab == true)
										{
											pcsP = ifnull(row.Required_Pieces,0) - (ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Waste,0) + ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Raw,0));
											if(pcsP > 0)
											{
												pinPcsLeft = pinPcsLeft + pcsP;
											}
										}
									}
								}
							}
						}
					}

					// Waste credit this pass's rows could still take. Pinned
					// picks for THIS item plus the free pool, and never more than
					// is actually left unspent. Subtracted from the piece budget
					// for the same reason the unpinned line subtracts
					// piecesFromWaste: cloth is not needed for a piece an offcut
					// is about to cover.
					wForPin = ifnull(wPinTot.get(passPin),"0").toString().trim();
					if(wForPin == "")
					{
						wForPin = "0";
					}
					wAvail = wFree + wForPin.toLong();
					if(wAvail > fanWaste)
					{
						wAvail = fanWaste;
					}

					//------------------------------------------------------
					// HOW MUCH COMES OFF THIS LOT.
					//
					// Recomputed here, per pass, from the pieces still owed at
					// this moment - NOT carved out of a budget worked out once
					// for the whole issue.
					//
					// That distinction is the whole reason this is a loop.
					// Cloth is usable only in whole marker rows, and each lot
					// is cut on its own, so the part-row at the end of one lot
					// cannot be finished with cloth from the next. A budget
					// computed once, as if the metres were one continuous
					// piece, is therefore SHORT by up to a row per extra lot -
					// and the shortfall is the stranded-piece failure CLAUDE.md
					// already records: the item never leaves Awaiting_Material,
					// the plan never reaches Material Ready, and pressing Issue
					// again does nothing because the "nothing outstanding" test
					// needs both metres and pieces to be spent.
					//
					// 100 pieces at 2 per row is 50 rows = 27.50m in one piece.
					// Split 20 + 7.50 it yields 36 + 13 = 49 rows, 98 pieces.
					// Budgeting the second pass from the 28 pieces still owed
					// asks for 7.70m instead, gets 14 rows, and closes it.
					//
					// So a split issue can spend slightly MORE metres than the
					// plan estimated. That is the documented model, not a leak:
					// Required_Qty is a pre-waste estimate and rounds up once,
					// while every separate cut rounds up again.
					//------------------------------------------------------
					//----------------------------------------------------------
					// IS THIS PASS A STACK OF PIECES RATHER THAN A ROLL?
					//
					// Printed cloth comes back as discrete full-width pieces and
					// its yield is NOT its metres. Each piece is cut on its own,
					// so it gives floor(len/cutL) rows and strands the tail below
					// one cut length - on EVERY piece. Three 3.00 m pieces are
					// 9.00 m and yield 15 rows of a 55 cm cut, where 9.00
					// continuous metres would give 16. Crediting that 16th row is
					// a piece nobody can cut: the requirement closes early, the
					// item never leaves Awaiting_Material, and pressing Issue
					// again does nothing because the "nothing outstanding" test
					// needs both metres and pieces spent.
					//
					// So for a pieces pass NOTHING comes off the metres. The
					// metres come from the pieces, and so does the yield.
					//
					// RE-READ OFF THE FORM, never trusted from the payload. A
					// Custom API is callable from anywhere, and a payload naming
					// its own lengths could book cloth that does not exist.
					//
					// pcSpec is empty for every roll lot, so all of this is
					// unreachable on the flow that existed before printing.
					//----------------------------------------------------------
					passIsPieces = false;
					pcYield = 0;
					pcMetres = 0.0;
					pcSpec = ifnull(passPieces.get(passId),"").toString().trim();

					if(isFab == true && pcSpec != "" && cutW > 0 && cutL > 0)
					{
						for each  pcTok in pcSpec.toList(",")
						{
							pcBits = pcTok.toString().trim().toList(":");
							if(pcBits.size() == 2)
							{
								pcWantId = pcBits.get(0).toString().trim();
								pcWantN = pcBits.get(1).toString().trim().toDecimal();

								if(pcWantId != "" && pcWantN > 0)
								{
									for each  fpChk in Fabric_Piece[ID == pcWantId.toLong()]
									{
										fpLotTxt = ifnull(fpChk.Lot,"").toString().trim();
										fpStat = ifnull(fpChk.Piece_Status,"").toString().trim();
										fpStateTxt = ifnull(fpChk.State,"").toString().trim();

										fpcS = ifnull(fpChk.Piece_Count,"0").toString().trim();
										if(fpcS == "")
										{
											fpcS = "0";
										}
										fplS = ifnull(fpChk.Piece_Length_Cm,"0").toString().trim();
										if(fplS == "")
										{
											fplS = "0";
										}
										fpwS = ifnull(fpChk.Piece_Width_Cm,"0").toString().trim();
										if(fpwS == "")
										{
											fpwS = "0";
										}

										// EVERY ONE OF THESE IS A REFUSAL, NOT A TRIM.
										// Silently taking less is what produced handovers
										// that looked complete and were not.
										if(fpLotTxt != passLot)
										{
											lotErr = matName + ": a piece in this handover is not on the lot it names";
										}
										else if(fpStat != "Available")
										{
											lotErr = matName + ": a piece in this handover is no longer available";
										}
										else if(fpStateTxt != "Wash")
										{
											lotErr = matName + ": a greige piece cannot be issued - it has to be washed first";
										}
										else if(pcWantN > fpcS.toDecimal())
										{
											lotErr = matName + ": only " + fpcS.toDecimal() + " of that piece are on the rack";
										}
										else
										{
											// floor(W/cutW) * floor(L/cutL) - the same
											// arithmetic an offcut uses, and correct for a
											// full-width piece because grain is fixed and a
											// piece is never rotated.
											pcAcross = (fpwS.toDecimal() / cutW).floor();
											pcAlong = (fplS.toDecimal() / cutL).floor();

											if(pcAcross > 0 && pcAlong > 0)
											{
												pcYield = pcYield + (pcAcross * pcAlong * pcWantN);
											}

											// A PIECE GOES OUT WHOLE. Its metres leave the
											// lot even where this cut cannot use all of it -
											// he cannot hand over half a piece, and the
											// remainder comes back as an offcut.
											pcMetres = pcMetres + ((fplS.toDecimal() * pcWantN) / 100);
											passIsPieces = true;
										}
									}
								}
							}
						}
					}

					thisQty = 0.0;
					if(isFab == true)
					{
						lpWantS = ifnull(lotWant.get(passId),"0").toString().trim();
						if(lpWantS == "")
						{
							lpWantS = "0";
						}
						thisQty = lpWantS.toDecimal();

						capQty = 0.0;
						if(canPiece == true)
						{
							pcsLeft = outPieces - piecesFromWaste - piecesFromRawTotal;
							if(passPin != "")
							{
								// The scan above already read what earlier passes
								// booked onto the rows, so there is nothing to
								// subtract here but the waste still to be placed.
								pcsLeft = pinPcsLeft - wAvail;
							}
							if(pcsLeft > 0)
							{
								// * 1.0 first - an integer quotient truncates
								// before .ceil() sees it, budgeting a row short.
								rowsCap = ((pcsLeft * 1.0) / perRowP).ceil();
								capQty = (rowsCap * cutL) / 100;
							}
						}
						else
						{
							// No piece data to count with - a row planned before
							// Required_Pieces existed, or a fabric whose width was
							// never recorded. Fall back to the metres balance.
							capQty = outstanding - issuedTotal;
							if(passPin != "")
							{
								// Live off the rows again, so no issuedTotal to
								// take off - what earlier passes gave this item is
								// already in its Issued_Qty.
								capQty = pinQtyLeft;
							}
						}

						if(thisQty > capQty)
						{
							thisQty = capQty;
						}

						//--------------------------------------------------
						// WHOLE MARKER ROWS ONLY.
						//
						// A row consumes cutL off the roll, so any part of a row
						// is cloth that cannot yield one piece of this cut. Let
						// 5.00m through against a 55cm cut and 9 rows come off
						// it - the odd 5cm walks out with the supervisor as a
						// full-width strip and is binned.
						//
						// Rounded DOWN, so the remainder stays on the roll in the
						// store where the next issue can use it. The widget snaps
						// as he types, but this is a Custom API and is callable
						// from anywhere, so the ledger enforces it too.
						//
						// It also makes piecesFromRaw exact rather than merely
						// guarded: the floor below can no longer discard anything,
						// because there is nothing left to discard.
						//--------------------------------------------------
						if(cutL > 0)
						{
							rowsFit = ((thisQty * 100) / cutL).floor();
							thisQty = (rowsFit * cutL) / 100;
						}

						// A PIECES PASS OVERRIDES ALL OF THAT.
						//
						// The metres are the pieces' own metres - not the
						// payload's figure, not a budget, and NOT snapped to
						// whole marker rows. A piece is issued whole: snapping
						// 9.00 m of three 3.00 m pieces down to 8.80 would send
						// out cloth the lot never lost, and there is no such
						// thing as 0.20 m of a piece to leave behind.
						//
						// The budget still did its job above - it is what the
						// widget sized the piece pick against - but it cannot
						// trim a physical object.
						if(passIsPieces == true)
						{
							thisQty = pcMetres;
						}
					}
					else
					{
						thisQty = issueQty - issuedTotal;
					}

					if(thisQty < 0)
					{
						thisQty = 0.0;
					}

					// Deluge has no reliable break; the body is guarded instead.
					// A lot whose share came out at zero - everything already
					// covered - costs one comparison and moves no stock.
					//
					// fanWaste keeps the body alive for a pass with no metres at
					// all, which is what a requirement covered entirely by
					// offcuts looks like. Without it the pieces move and nothing
					// books them.
					if(thisQty > 0 || fanWaste > 0)
					{

					//------------------------------------------------------
					// How many pieces did THIS LOT'S cloth cover? Off its own
					// metres, never off the combined total.
					//------------------------------------------------------
					piecesFromRaw = 0;
					if(canPiece == true)
					{
						perRow = perRowP;
						rowsIssued = ((thisQty * 100) / cutL).floor();
						piecesFromRaw = perRow * rowsIssued;

						// THE ONE LINE THIS WHOLE FEATURE EXISTS FOR.
						//
						// On a stack of pieces the division above is WRONG and
						// wrong in the dangerous direction - it credits more
						// pieces than the cloth can yield, because it treats the
						// tails as if they joined up. pcYield was summed per
						// piece from the pieces themselves, so it is the truth.
						if(passIsPieces == true)
						{
							piecesFromRaw = pcYield;
						}
						stillNeeded = outPieces - piecesFromWaste - piecesFromRawTotal;
						if(passPin != "")
						{
							// Same budget the metres were capped against a few
							// lines up, and it has to be the same one: credit this
							// pass with pieces its rows cannot absorb and the next
							// pass reads them as already covered.
							stillNeeded = pinPcsLeft - wAvail;
						}
						if(piecesFromRaw > stillNeeded)
						{
							piecesFromRaw = stillNeeded;
						}
						if(piecesFromRaw < 0)
						{
							piecesFromRaw = 0;
						}
					}
					piecesFromRawTotal = piecesFromRawTotal + piecesFromRaw;
					issuedTotal = issuedTotal + thisQty;

				//----------------------------------------------------------
				// Pass 3 - fan this lot's qty across the pending rows.
				//
				// "Pending rows" is narrowed by the pin: a pass the widget named
				// an item for may only reach that item's rows. Same kind of test
				// as cut size and Source below - a row belonging to another item
				// is not this pass's row, however well the material matches - and
				// it is the one that stops an order straddling two lots.
				//----------------------------------------------------------
				toFan = thisQty;
				fanRaw = piecesFromRaw;
				for each  plan in plans
				{
					for each  row in Material_Requirement[Plan == plan.ID]
					{
						if(row.Material != null && row.Material.toString() == matId)
						{
							sizeOk2 = true;
							if(isFab == true)
							{
								if(ifnull(row.Cut_Size_Width,0) != cutW || ifnull(row.Cut_Size_Length,0) != cutL)
								{
									sizeOk2 = false;
								}
							}
							// Same rule as cut size: a row of the WRONG KIND is not this line's
							// row, however well the material matches. Empty reads as "Plan".
							rowSrc2 = ifnull(row.Source,"").toString().trim();
							if(rowSrc2 != "Reissue")
							{
								rowSrc2 = "Plan";
							}
							if(rowSrc2 != srcWanted)
							{
								sizeOk2 = false;
							}
							// WHOSE ROW THIS IS. Lookup fields hold record ids, so
							// this compares an id with the id the payload carried.
							// An unpinned pass leaves it alone and fans exactly as
							// it always did, which is what keeps an older widget
							// working.
							rowPin = ifnull(row.Plan_Item,"").toString().trim();
							if(passPin != "" && rowPin != passPin)
							{
								sizeOk2 = false;
							}
							if(sizeOk2 == true)
							{
								if(toFan > 0)
								{
									reqStr2 = ifnull(row.Required_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
									if(reqStr2 == "")
									{
										reqStr2 = "0";
									}
									issStr2 = ifnull(row.Issued_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
									if(issStr2 == "")
									{
										issStr2 = "0";
									}
									alreadyIssued = issStr2.toDecimal();
									rowRem = reqStr2.toDecimal() - alreadyIssued;

									//------------------------------------------
									// WHAT THIS ROW STILL NEEDS, IN PIECES.
									//
									// For a piece-tracked row this REPLACES the
									// metres balance rather than merely raising
									// it, and both directions matter.
									//
									// UP, or the fix upstairs leaks. The budget is
									// piece-derived and can exceed the sum of
									// Required_Qty - Issued_Qty across these rows,
									// so the fan would run out of room, leave
									// toFan unspent, and the difference would come
									// off real stock with no Issued_Qty anywhere
									// to book it.
									//
									// DOWN, or a lot's cloth lands on the wrong
									// item. Required_Qty is the PRE-WASTE estimate
									// and is never topped up or trimmed, so a row
									// whose pieces were partly covered by offcuts
									// keeps a metres residue it does not need. On
									// a one-lot issue that was cosmetic. Once the
									// passes are per lot it is not: the residue
									// swallows the NEXT lot's cloth, and the
									// Issue_Lines row records that item as having
									// come off a lot it never touched.
									//
									//   20 pieces, 6 already off offcuts, 2/row:
									//   needs 7 rows = 3.85m, but Required_Qty
									//   still says 5.50m. Pass 1 fills it from L1.
									//   Pass 2's first 1.65m then lands on the
									//   SAME row - off lot L2 - and the item is
									//   stamped with two tones it never had.
									//
									// Two tones on one item is the exact thing the
									// lot system exists to prevent, and it would
									// also route that item's offcuts back to the
									// wrong lot.
									//
									// It cannot under-absorb: the sum of the
									// per-row ceilings is always at least the
									// aggregate ceiling, because
									// ceil(a/n) + ceil(b/n) >= ceil((a+b)/n).
									//
									// Required_Pieces == 0 means the row is not
									// piece-tracked - planned before the field
									// existed - and it keeps the metres balance it
									// has always used.
									//------------------------------------------
									if(isFab == true && cutW > 0 && cutL > 0 && fabricWidthCm >= cutW)
									{
										perRowF = (fabricWidthCm / cutW).floor();
										rowPcsReq = ifnull(row.Required_Pieces,0);
										rowPcsRem = rowPcsReq - (ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Waste,0) + ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Raw,0));
										if(perRowF > 0 && rowPcsReq > 0)
										{
											rowCap = 0.0;
											if(rowPcsRem > 0)
											{
												rowsF = ((rowPcsRem * 1.0) / perRowF).ceil();
												rowCap = (rowsF * cutL) / 100;
											}
											rowRem = rowCap;
										}
									}

									if(rowRem > 0)
									{
										give = rowRem;
										if(toFan < rowRem)
										{
											give = toFan;
										}
										row.Issued_Qty = alreadyIssued + give;
										toFan = toFan - give;

										// Same pass that writes the balance records
										// the event, so the two can never disagree.
										if(give > 0)
										{
											if(primaryPlanId == 0)
											{
												primaryPlanId = plan.ID;
											}

											//--------------------------------------
											// STAMP THE TONE, ONCE AND FOR EVER.
											//
											// Issued_Lot is what this row was first
											// cut from, and every later issue against
											// it - a reissue for a ruined panel, a
											// remake three weeks on - has to match or
											// the finished pieces do not.
											//
											// WRITTEN ONLY WHEN EMPTY. The first lot
											// decides; a second write would rewrite
											// history, and history is the whole value
											// of the field. It is also what the store
											// screen reads back to pin the next issue,
											// so overwriting it would quietly move the
											// target rather than fail visibly.
											//
											// Metres only. A pass with no lot behind it
											// is non-fabric, which has no tone.
											//--------------------------------------
											if(passLot != "")
											{
												curLot = ifnull(row.Issued_Lot,"").toString().trim();
												if(curLot == "")
												{
													row.Issued_Lot = passLot.toLong();
												}
											}

											lnMap = Map();
											lnMap.put("req",row.ID);
											lnMap.put("planItem",row.Plan_Item);
											// WHICH CLOTH crossed the counter. The
											// requirement row records metres and has
											// never recorded which lot they were, so
											// this line is the only place that can
											// answer it - and receipt, disputes and
											// offcut provenance all have to ask.
											lnMap.put("lot",passLot);
											lnMap.put("note",ifnull(noteOf.get(passId),"").toString());
											lnMap.put("fromLot",ifnull(fromOf.get(passId),"").toString());
											lnMap.put("qty",give);
											lnMap.put("unit",ifnull(row.Unit,"").toString());
											lnMap.put("cutW",cutW);
											lnMap.put("cutL",cutL);
											lnMap.put("matId",matId);
											lnMap.put("matName",matName);
											lnMap.put("matDisp",matDisp);
											issueLines.add(lnMap);
										}
									}
								}

								if(isFab == true && (fanWaste > 0 || fanRaw > 0))
								{
									alreadyW = ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Waste,0);
									alreadyR = ifnull(row.Pieces_From_Raw,0);
									pRem2 = ifnull(row.Required_Pieces,0) - (alreadyW + alreadyR);
									if(pRem2 > 0)
									{
										// Waste first - it is the scarcer,
										// already-paid-for source, so it should
										// close the oldest rows.
										//
										// WHAT THIS ROW MAY DRAW ON is its own
										// pinned picks plus the unpinned pool - a
										// remnant the widget allocated to another
										// item is not on offer here, however
										// short this row is. With no pin anywhere
										// the free pool is everything and this is
										// the old `giveW = fanWaste` exactly.
										//
										// fanWaste is still the ceiling: the two
										// buckets were never trimmed to the
										// "more cuts than needed" clamp above, so
										// only the running total knows what is
										// really left.
										pinHave = ifnull(wPinTot.get(rowPin),"0").toString().trim();
										if(pinHave == "")
										{
											pinHave = "0";
										}
										giveW = wFree + pinHave.toLong();
										if(giveW > fanWaste)
										{
											giveW = fanWaste;
										}
										if(giveW > pRem2)
										{
											giveW = pRem2;
										}
										if(giveW > 0)
										{
											row.Pieces_From_Waste = alreadyW + giveW;
											fanWaste = fanWaste - giveW;
											pRem2 = pRem2 - giveW;

											// Spend the pinned pieces before the
											// free ones. They can go nowhere else,
											// while the pool can still serve any
											// row the fan reaches after this.
											useP = giveW;
											if(useP > pinHave.toLong())
											{
												useP = pinHave.toLong();
											}
											if(useP > 0)
											{
												wPinTot.put(rowPin,pinHave.toLong() - useP);
											}
											wFree = wFree - (giveW - useP);
											if(wFree < 0)
											{
												wFree = 0;
											}

											// Record what physical cloth the
											// supervisor actually receives - the
											// counters above say how much is
											// owed, not what is in his hands.
											if(wasteLogged == false)
											{
												wasteLogged = true;
												for each  pkRaw3 in picks
												{
													pk3 = pkRaw3.toMap();
													wId3 = ifnull(pk3.get("wasteId"),"").toString().trim();
													pcs3 = ifnull(pk3.get("pieces"),"0").toString().trim();
													if(pcs3 == "")
													{
														pcs3 = "0";
													}
													nPcs3 = pcs3.toDecimal().toLong();
													if(nPcs3 > 0 && pkYield.get(wId3) != null)
													{
														// STEP 4b: an Issued movement replaces the
														// Waste_Issued subform row. No Received_Count
														// here - receipt is its own movement pointing
														// back at this one, so a short receipt leaves
														// both numbers and both dates on the record
														// instead of one mutable counter.
														newMvId = insert into Waste_Movement
														[
															Movement_Type="Issued"
															Piece_Width=pkW.get(wId3)
															Piece_Length=pkL.get(wId3)
															Piece_Count=nPcs3
															Cut_Size_Width=cutW
															Cut_Size_Length=cutL
															Pieces_Yielded=nPcs3 * pkYield.get(wId3)
															Moved_On=zoho.currentdate
															Added_User=zoho.loginuser
														];

														// Lookups after the insert - a null cannot go
														// in the insert block.
														for each  mv in Waste_Movement[ID == newMvId.toLong()]
														{
															mv.Waste_Piece = wId3.toLong();
															mv.Plan = plan.ID;
															mv.Plan_Item = row.Plan_Item;
															mv.Moved_By = supId;
														}
													}
												}
											}
										}
										giveR = fanRaw;
										if(giveR > pRem2)
										{
											giveR = pRem2;
										}
										if(giveR > 0)
										{
											row.Pieces_From_Raw = alreadyR + giveR;
											fanRaw = fanRaw - giveR;
										}
									}
								}
							}
						}
					}
				}

					//------------------------------------------------------
					// Move THIS LOT'S stock, in the same pass that booked it.
					//
					// The lot holds the truth; Raw_Material below holds the
					// maintained total. Both move for the same metres, here and
					// there, so they cannot drift apart.
					//
					// Washed only - fabric never leaves the shelf unwashed, and
					// the validation above already refused a lot that could not
					// cover this from Wash_Quantity.
					//------------------------------------------------------
					if(passLot != "")
					{
						for each  lotMv in Raw_Material_Lot[ID == passLot.toLong()]
						{
							lmwS = ifnull(lotMv.Wash_Quantity,"0").toString().trim();
							if(lmwS == "")
							{
								lmwS = "0";
							}
							lmtS = ifnull(lotMv.In_Transit_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
							if(lmtS == "")
							{
								lmtS = "0";
							}
							lotMv.Wash_Quantity = lmwS.toDecimal() - thisQty;
							lotMv.In_Transit_Qty = lmtS.toDecimal() + thisQty;
						}

						//--------------------------------------------------
						// AND THE PIECES THEMSELVES COME OFF THE RACK.
						//
						// The lot's metres moved above; on a Pieces lot those
						// metres are the MAINTAINED SUM of these rows, so
						// leaving the rows alone would make the header and its
						// pieces disagree - the lot claiming it had given cloth
						// away while every piece still sat there to be offered
						// again on the next render.
						//
						// Decremented, never deleted: the row is the provenance
						// of the cloth and of the print job it came from. A row
						// reaching zero simply stops being Available stock,
						// which is what getStoreMaterialRequirements filters on.
						if(passIsPieces == true)
						{
							for each  pcMvTok in pcSpec.toList(",")
							{
								pcMvBits = pcMvTok.toString().trim().toList(":");
								if(pcMvBits.size() == 2)
								{
									pcMvId = pcMvBits.get(0).toString().trim();
									pcMvN = pcMvBits.get(1).toString().trim().toDecimal();

									if(pcMvId != "" && pcMvN > 0)
									{
										for each  fpMv in Fabric_Piece[ID == pcMvId.toLong()]
										{
											fpMvS = ifnull(fpMv.Piece_Count,"0").toString().trim();
											if(fpMvS == "")
											{
												fpMvS = "0";
											}
											fpMvLeft = fpMvS.toDecimal() - pcMvN;
											if(fpMvLeft < 0)
											{
												// Cannot happen - the validation
												// above refuses it - but a count
												// below zero would be a phantom
												// the allocator could never
												// reconcile, so it is clamped and
												// said out loud rather than left.
												info "issueMaterials: piece " + pcMvId + " would go negative - clamped";
												fpMvLeft = 0;
											}
											fpMv.Piece_Count = fpMvLeft;
										}
									}
								}
							}
						}
					}

					}
					// end of the thisQty > 0 guard
				}
				// end of the per-lot pass loop

				// Every pass has run; this is what actually left the shelf.
				// issueQty was only ever the pre-pass estimate, and for a split
				// across lots the passes can legitimately total more.
				issueQty = issuedTotal;

				//----------------------------------------------------------
				// Move the stock, do NOT consume it.
				//
				// The shelf figure drops so the same cloth cannot be promised
				// to a second supervisor, but the company still owns it - it is
				// on a trolley, not in a garment. It is consumed only when the
				// supervisor confirms receipt, and only for the amount he
				// actually confirms.
				//
				// So Wash_Quantity/Quantity now means "on the shelf", not "what
				// we own". Total holdings = on hand + in transit + disputed.
				//----------------------------------------------------------
				balance = available - issueQty;
				if(isFab == true)
				{
					matRec.Wash_Quantity = balance;
				}
				else
				{
					matRec.Quantity = balance;
				}

				transStr = ifnull(matRec.In_Transit_Qty,"0").toString().trim();
				if(transStr == "")
				{
					transStr = "0";
				}
				matRec.In_Transit_Qty = transStr.toDecimal() + issueQty;

				if(okJson != "")
				{
					okJson = okJson + ",";
				}
				// IDs forced to string - 18-digit ids break JSON.parse otherwise.
				okJson = okJson + "{\"materialId\":\"" + matId + "\",\"sku\":\"" + matName + "\",\"qty\":" + issueQty + ",\"balance\":" + balance + ",\"wastePiecesIssued\":" + physicalPcs + ",\"piecesFromWaste\":" + piecesFromWaste + ",\"piecesFromRaw\":" + piecesFromRawTotal + "}";

				if(issueQty < askQty)
				{
					if(errJson != "")
					{
						errJson = errJson + ",";
					}
					errJson = errJson + "\"" + matName + ": only " + issueQty + " was needed, issued that instead of " + askQty + "\"";
				}
			}
		}
	}

	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// Write the handover record. Deliberately in its own try/catch: by this
	// point stock has already been decremented and balances already moved, so a
	// failure to LOG the handover must never fail the handover itself. Losing
	// the audit row is bad; rolling back cloth that is physically on the counter
	// is worse, and Deluge has no transaction to roll back with anyway.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	try
	{
		if(issueLines.size() > 0)
		{
			//------------------------------------------------------------
			// THE VOUCHER NUMBER. One per handover, because one handover is
			// one record - the store issues again, that is a new record and a
			// new number.
			//
			// GENERATED HERE, NOT BY A FORM WORKFLOW. An "on add" workflow
			// using input.Voucher_No fires only when a human opens the Creator
			// form and types a record. Nothing ever does: every Material_Issue
			// in this app is created by the insert below, so a workflow would
			// leave every voucher blank. Same reason Plan_No is built inside
			// createProductionPlans rather than on the form.
			//
			// SORTED ON ID, NOT ON Voucher_No, AND THAT IS NOT A DETAIL.
			//
			// "sort by Voucher_No desc" is a TEXT sort. Zero padding makes it
			// agree with the numeric order only while the width stays the same -
			// the moment SIV-100000 exists beside SIV-99999, text order puts
			// SIV-9... above SIV-1..., the highest reads as 99999, and the next
			// handover is issued SIV-100000 for the SECOND time. A duplicate
			// voucher number that nothing would notice until two transfer orders
			// quoted it.
			//
			// Record ids are monotonic, so the newest row always holds the
			// highest number whatever its width, and the ceiling disappears.
			//
			// TEN ROWS, NOT ONE. Sorting by id means the top row is the NEWEST,
			// not the highest - and a row with an empty Voucher_No at the top
			// would restart numbering at 1 and duplicate everything. Nothing
			// writes a blank today, but a record added by hand in Creator would,
			// and so would this very field the day before it was filled in.
			// Walking a few rows and keeping the largest costs nothing and
			// cannot be tripped by one bad row.
			//
			// Bounded either way. The plan numberer scans every plan ever to
			// read one integer and is listed in docs/scaling.md for it; there
			// was no reason to copy that.
			//
			// NOT SAFE AGAINST TWO STORE PEOPLE ISSUING IN THE SAME INSTANT -
			// both would read the same last number. There is one store counter
			// and one person at it, and Deluge has no sequence to lock; if that
			// ever changes this needs a different mechanism, not a retry.
			//------------------------------------------------------------
			// sort by on its own assignment, never inline in a for-each header.
			lastVouchers = Material_Issue[ID != 0] sort by ID desc range from 1 to 10;

			lastVNum = 0;

			for each  lv in lastVouchers
			{
				lvTxt = ifnull(lv.Voucher_No,"").toString().trim();

				if(lvTxt != "")
				{
					// Split rather than substring - toList/get is the string
					// idiom this app already relies on, and it does not care how
					// many dashes a hand-edited number picked up.
					vParts = lvTxt.toList("-");

					if(vParts.size() > 1)
					{
						vNumTxt = vParts.get(vParts.size() - 1).trim();

						if(vNumTxt.isNumber() == true)
						{
							vSeen = vNumTxt.toLong();

							if(vSeen > lastVNum)
							{
								lastVNum = vSeen;
							}
						}
					}
				}
			}

			nextVNum = lastVNum + 1;

			if(nextVNum < 10)
			{
				vPad = "0000" + nextVNum;
			}
			else if(nextVNum < 100)
			{
				vPad = "000" + nextVNum;
			}
			else if(nextVNum < 1000)
			{
				vPad = "00" + nextVNum;
			}
			else if(nextVNum < 10000)
			{
				vPad = "0" + nextVNum;
			}
			else
			{
				vPad = "" + nextVNum;
			}

			voucherNo = "SIV-" + vPad;

			// Transfer_Status is set so the state is READABLE on the record.
			// Nothing depends on it: postTransferOrders reads every recent
			// handover and filters in code, because a Creator criteria drops an
			// empty field whether the test is "==" or "!=" - which is how a
			// voucher with a blank status stayed invisible to two different
			// queries in a row.
			//
			// NO COMMENTS INSIDE THE BLOCK BELOW. A Deluge insert-into field
			// list is parsed specially and a "//" line in it is a syntax error -
			// reported against the "try" a thousand lines above, with no hint
			// that an insert is involved at all.
			newIssueId = insert into Material_Issue
			[
				Voucher_No=voucherNo
				Issue_Date=zoho.currentdate
				Issue_Status="Issued"
				Transfer_Status="Pending"
				Added_User=zoho.loginuser
			];

			for each  mi in Material_Issue[ID == newIssueId.toLong()]
			{
				// Lookups after the insert - a null cannot go in the insert block.
				if(primaryPlanId != 0)
				{
					mi.Plan = primaryPlanId;
				}
				mi.Issued_To = supId;
				// Set here rather than in the insert block - a Time field wants
				// "HH:mm", and this whole block is already inside its own catch.
				mi.Issue_Time = zoho.currenttime.toString("HH:mm");

				for each  lnRaw in issueLines
				{
					liRow = Material_Issue.Issue_Lines();
					liRow.Material=lnRaw.get("matId").toLong();

					// Material_Name gets the readable NAME, not the SKU it used to
					// be given. The field finally says what it contains.
					//
					// The SKU is NOT written to a second field - there is no text
					// field on this subform to hold it. Material is a LOOKUP whose
					// display column happens to be the SKU, which is why Creator's
					// own form shows "RM-00005" there; Deluge reading that field
					// gets the record id instead. getStoreIssueHistory resolves the
					// code from a catalogue map it builds in one query, so nothing
					// here has to.
					liRow.Material_Name=lnRaw.get("matDisp");
					liRow.Qty=lnRaw.get("qty");
					liRow.Unit=lnRaw.get("unit");
					liRow.Cut_Size_Width=lnRaw.get("cutW");
					liRow.Cut_Size_Length=lnRaw.get("cutL");

					// Nothing confirmed yet. receiveMaterials settles against
					// this, oldest line first, to know which lot to take the
					// in-transit cloth off - so it has to start at a real 0
					// rather than the EMPTY a Creator numeric otherwise holds.
					liRow.Settled_Qty=0;

					lotTxt = ifnull(lnRaw.get("lot"),"").toString().trim();
					if(lotTxt != "")
					{
						liRow.Lot=lotTxt.toLong();
					}

					// Written only when a tone override actually happened, so an
					// ordinary handover line looks exactly as it always did.
					noteTxt = ifnull(lnRaw.get("note"),"").toString().trim();
					if(noteTxt != "")
					{
						fromTxt = ifnull(lnRaw.get("fromLot"),"").toString().trim();
						if(fromTxt != "")
						{
							liRow.Lot_Override_From=fromTxt.toLong();
						}
						liRow.Lot_Override_Note=noteTxt;
					}
					if(lnRaw.get("req") != null)
					{
						liRow.Requirement=lnRaw.get("req");
					}
					if(lnRaw.get("planItem") != null)
					{
						liRow.Plan_Item=lnRaw.get("planItem");
					}
					mi.Issue_Lines.insert(liRow);
				}
			}
		}
	}
	catch (e2)
	{
		// Not returned to the widget - the issue succeeded, and telling the store
		// person "handover not logged" would only make him issue it again.
		info "Material_Issue not written: " + e2.toString();
	}

	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// TELL THE SUPERVISOR HIS MATERIAL IS READY.
	//
	// ONE mail per handover, not per material - a handover of eight materials is
	// one trip to the counter, and eight mails describing it would be eight
	// chances to stop reading them.
	//
	// Its own try/catch, and last. The cloth is already on the counter and the
	// stock is already moved by this point; a mail that fails - quota, a
	// transient error - must never make a completed handover look failed, and
	// must never leave the store person issuing it a second time.
	//
	// ADDRESSING, and its one honest limitation: there is no Email field on
	// Employee, so this cannot reach the supervisor directly. It goes to the
	// admin address and NAMES him in the subject and the body, which is the
	// agreed behaviour for a missing address - the notification is never lost,
	// and the gap is visible in every mail until it is closed.
	//   TO SWITCH ON once Employee.Email exists: read it in the supName loop
	//   below and use it as `to`, keeping this address as the fallback.
	//------------------------------------------------------------------------
	notifyTo = "gurdhian.helios@gmail.com";

	try
	{
		if(issueLines.size() > 0)
		{
			supName = "";
			for each  supEmp in Employee[ID == supId]
			{
				supName = ifnull(supEmp.Employee_Name,"").toString().replaceAll("\"","'");
			}
			if(supName == "")
			{
				supName = "Supervisor";
			}

			// One row per MATERIAL, summed. issueLines carries a row per plan
			// requirement, so the same material appears several times when one
			// handover feeds several orders - and he carried it once. Same rule
			// his own Receive screen states: one line per physical thing.
			nFont = "-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Inter,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif";
			nTh = "padding:8px 12px;background:#f1f5f9;border-bottom:1px solid #e2e8f0;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#64748b;text-align:left;font-weight:600";
			nThR = "padding:8px 12px;background:#f1f5f9;border-bottom:1px solid #e2e8f0;font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.04em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#64748b;text-align:right;font-weight:600";
			nTd = "padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #eef2f6;font-size:13px;color:#0f172a";
			nTdR = "padding:10px 12px;border-bottom:1px solid #eef2f6;font-size:13px;color:#0f172a;text-align:right;font-weight:600";

			mailKeys = List();
			mailQty = Map();
			mailUnit = Map();

			for each  mlRaw in issueLines
			{
				// Name AND unit in the key: the same material in Mtr and in Pcs
				// is two different things and must never be added together.
				mlName = ifnull(mlRaw.get("matDisp"),"").toString();
				mlUnitTxt = ifnull(mlRaw.get("unit"),"").toString();
				mlKey = mlName + " | " + mlUnitTxt;

				mlHave = ifnull(mailQty.get(mlKey),"").toString().trim();
				if(mlHave == "")
				{
					mailKeys.add(mlKey);
					mailQty.put(mlKey,ifnull(mlRaw.get("qty"),0));
					mailUnit.put(mlKey,mlUnitTxt);
				}
				else
				{
					mailQty.put(mlKey,mlHave.toDecimal() + ifnull(mlRaw.get("qty"),0));
				}
			}

			rowsHtml = "";
			for each  mlKey2 in mailKeys
			{
				mlShow = mlKey2.substring(0,mlKey2.lastIndexOf(" | "));
				rowsHtml = rowsHtml + "<tr><td style='" + nTd + "'>" + mlShow + "</td><td style='" + nTdR + "'>" + mailQty.get(mlKey2) + " <span style='color:#64748b;font-weight:400'>" + ifnull(mailUnit.get(mlKey2),"") + "</span></td></tr>";
			}

			inner = "<div style='font-size:14px;color:#0f172a'><b>" + supName + "</b>, the store has issued material against your orders. It is ready to collect.</div>";
			inner = inner + "<table role='presentation' width='100%' cellpadding='0' cellspacing='0' border='0' style='border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:14px;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-radius:6px'>";
			inner = inner + "<tr><th style='" + nTh + "'>Material</th><th style='" + nThR + "'>Quantity</th></tr>";
			inner = inner + rowsHtml + "</table>";
			inner = inner + "<div style='font-size:12px;color:#64748b;padding-top:14px'>Confirm what you actually received on the <b>Receive</b> tab. Anything short of this goes to a dispute rather than quietly disappearing - stock is only consumed when you confirm it.</div>";

			mailHtml = thisapp.buildNotificationEmail("Collect your material from the store","For " + supName + " &middot; " + zoho.currentdate.toString("dd MMM yyyy") + " " + zoho.currenttime.toString("HH:mm"),"#2563eb",inner);

			sendmail
			[
				from :zoho.adminuserid
				to :notifyTo
				subject :"Material ready to collect from the store"
				message :mailHtml
			]
			info "Issue notification sent for " + supName;
		}
	}
	catch (e3)
	{
		// Logged, never rethrown, never returned. The handover happened.
		info "Issue notification not sent: " + e3.toString();
	}

	}
	catch (e)
	{
		// Surfaces as an alert in the widget via the existing errors[] handling.
		return "{\"errors\":[\"DELUGE: " + e.toString().replaceAll("\"","'") + "\"],\"issued\":[]}";
	}

	// Built by hand - List.toString() does not reliably wrap in [].
	return "{\"errors\":[" + errJson + "],\"issued\":[" + okJson + "]}";
}
